Retain the contents of deleted and view-once messages, dimmed

Instead of destroying a message's contents when it is deleted for everyone or
consumed as view-once, keep them on disk and render them dimmed, above a notice
saying what happened.

A new `retainedAfterErase` message attribute is set alongside `isErased` for the
erase reasons we protect (delete-for-everyone and the view-once family), and the
attachment files are left on disk for those reasons. It rides along in the
`messages.json` blob, so no schema migration is needed.

Everything outside the renderer keeps upstream semantics: `isErased` is still
set, so full-text search, the media gallery and the tap-to-view deletion service
behave as before, and the backup exporter still writes a bare
`remoteDeletedMessage`. Retained contents are therefore local-only and do not
survive a backup/restore round-trip.

Messages that were erased before this build have no `retainedAfterErase` flag and
keep the upstream tombstone rendering.

Note: this deliberately ignores a request from the sender, who is told their
delete succeeded and has no way to know this build kept the message.
